{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-181269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/assets/f00499aad7b915267fe99bde8c13665a325cf6409e2e856466e9cdc90e4a8b8a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2x-3}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4a6c92f8b248cad26c90bde9b2c5481f29cb1e765b1079353614df07ba7c970"} {"question" "q-181269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=2x-3.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<2x-3\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=2x-3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le 2x-3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le 2x-3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4a6c92f8b248cad26c90bde9b2c5481f29cb1e765b1079353614df07ba7c970"} {"question" "q-181269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c96b83962fa33f4d452d615b872bc0b214fd830aeea0ad5fbf859068e97ba2fb"} {"question" "q-181269" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.t