2c5481f29cb1e765b1079353614df07ba7c970"} {"question" "q-181684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/assets/09df7202fbe0c9160e6c10dc10a813bc6b4b00aae7d95a01068dd4154fb5d929.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-2\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4a6c92f8b248cad26c90bde9b2c5481f29cb1e765b1079353614df07ba7c970"} {"question" "q-181684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\,y<x.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<x\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<y<x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4a6c92f8b248cad26c90bde9b2c5481f29cb1e765b1079353614df07ba7c970"} {"question" "q-181684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070909/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c96b83962fa33f4d452d615b872bc0b214fd830aeea0ad5fbf859068e97ba2fb"} {"question" "q-181684" "field" "field-1" "attribute" 